  "preview": "Forest rangers measure sapling heights in a protected forest and a recently replanted one. Both samples have nearly the same mean. Are the two forests really 'the same'?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Compare two populations using both center (mean) AND variability (MAD or visual spread). Recognize that two samples can share a mean and still describe very different populations.",
    "studentPrompt": "Two forests grow side-by-side. Cedar Hollow is a protected old-growth area. Maple Ridge was clear-cut and replanted 10 years ago. A ranger randomly samples 10 saplings from each forest and records their heights in centimeters.\n\nCedar Hollow: 58, 62, 60, 64, 59, 61, 63, 60, 62, 61\nMaple Ridge:  42, 78, 50, 70, 88, 35, 65, 55, 80, 47\n\n1. Find the mean height for each forest's sample. What do you notice?\n2. Find the mean absolute deviation (MAD) for each forest's sample. (Reminder: average the distances of each value from the mean.)\n3. Sketch a dot plot for each forest on the same number-line scale. What story do the plots tell that the means alone do not?\n4. A scientist claims, 'The two forests have populations with the same typical sapling height.' Do you agree? Make an inference, and support it with BOTH the centers and the variability.\n5. If you had to predict next year's tallest sapling, which forest would you bet on, and why?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "Two side-by-side forest scenes. On the left, Cedar Hollow shows tall trees of nearly identical height in a tidy row. On the right, Maple Ridge shows saplings of wildly different heights — some tall, some tiny, some medium.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"sky2\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#cfe6f2\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#f7f5e8\"/></linearGradient></defs><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"180\" fill=\"url(#sky2)\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"175\" width=\"240\" height=\"45\" fill=\"#6b8e4e\"/><rect x=\"240\" y=\"175\" width=\"240\" height=\"45\" fill=\"#a3a86b\"/><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#3b2f5e\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\" opacity=\"0.4\"/><g stroke=\"#4a3520\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"#4a3520\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"95\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"90\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"97\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"92\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"95\"/><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"90\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"94\"/></g><g fill=\"#3f6b3a\"><ellipse cx=\"30\" cy=\"90\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/><ellipse cx=\"60\" cy=\"85\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/><ellipse cx=\"90\" cy=\"92\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/><ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"87\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/><ellipse cx=\"150\" cy=\"90\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"85\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/><ellipse cx=\"210\" cy=\"89\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/></g><g stroke=\"#5a3f24\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"#5a3f24\"><line x1=\"260\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"150\"/><line x1=\"285\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"310\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"130\"/><line x1=\"335\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"335\" y2=\"80\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"40\"/><line x1=\"385\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"160\"/><line x1=\"410\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"410\" y2=\"105\"/><line x1=\"435\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"435\" y2=\"140\"/><line x1=\"460\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"55\"/></g><g fill=\"#7a9b4a\"><ellipse cx=\"260\" cy=\"148\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"9\"/><ellipse cx=\"285\" cy=\"58\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"18\"/><ellipse cx=\"310\" cy=\"128\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"12\"/><ellipse cx=\"335\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"15\"/><ellipse cx=\"360\" cy=\"38\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"20\"/><ellipse cx=\"385\" cy=\"158\" rx=\"6\" ry=\"8\"/><ellipse cx=\"410\" cy=\"103\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"14\"/><ellipse cx=\"435\" cy=\"138\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"10\"/><ellipse cx=\"460\" cy=\"53\" rx=\"13\" ry=\"17\"/></g><text x=\"120\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Cedar Hollow</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Maple Ridge</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#3b2f5e\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">old growth</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#3b2f5e\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">replanted 10 yrs ago</text></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Tell the story: 'Two forests. One protected for a hundred years. One replanted ten years ago after a clear-cut. A ranger measures saplings in each.' Show the picture. Read the two number lists out loud and let kids hear the difference. Send them to boards — do NOT pre-teach MAD. Let them invent or recall it.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: calculators",
      "Optional: graph paper for dot plots"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "mean",
      "mean-absolute-deviation",
      "variability",
      "comparing-populations",
      "inference-from-samples"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Solid BTC. Q1 is computational warm-up (low floor) and surfaces the surprise — the means are equal. Q2 is the diamond if students remember MAD. Q3 makes the difference visual. Q4 forces them to articulate that 'same center' ≠ 'same population.' Q5 is a tasty extension.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Both means come out to 61.0. Some groups will recompute, certain they made an error. Sit with the surprise.\n- Q2: Cedar Hollow MAD is small (~1.4). Maple Ridge MAD is large (~14.8). Some groups skip the absolute values and get zero — that's a teachable moment.\n- Q3: Cedar Hollow dots cluster tightly between 58–64. Maple Ridge dots spread from 35–88. The picture is instantly convincing.\n- Q4: Strong groups will write something like 'same average height, but very different populations — Cedar Hollow is uniform, Maple Ridge is highly variable.'\n- Q5: Maple Ridge — its spread reaches higher values. A few groups argue Cedar Hollow because its trees are predictably tall. Both reasonings are valid; the point is that 'tallest' is about the MAX, not the MEAN.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting absolute values when computing MAD (signed deviations sum to zero).\n- Concluding 'the forests are identical' because the means agree.\n- Confusing range with MAD. Range answers 'how far apart are the extremes?' MAD answers 'how far, on average, are the values from the center?'\n- Predicting Cedar Hollow has the next-year tallest sapling because 'its trees are taller on average' — they aren't; the AVERAGES are identical.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- The means are exactly equal. Does that mean the two populations are the same? What evidence convinced you otherwise?\n- A friend says, 'MAD is just the standard deviation for kids.' Is that true? When would MAD be enough? When wouldn't it be?\n- Maple Ridge is a replanted forest. Why might a replanted forest have more variable sapling heights than an old-growth one?",
    "extensions": "- Compute the median for each sample. Compare each median to its mean. In which forest do mean and median diverge more, and what does that tell you about the SHAPE of the data?\n- A third forest, Birch Bend, has a sample with mean 61 and MAD 7. Sketch a plausible dot plot for Birch Bend. Where does it sit between Cedar Hollow and Maple Ridge?\n- The ranger wants to detect a real difference between Cedar Hollow's TRUE mean and Maple Ridge's TRUE mean. Why does Maple Ridge need a larger sample for the ranger to be confident? Tie your answer to MAD.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Cedar Hollow mean = (58+62+60+64+59+61+63+60+62+61)/10 = 610/10 = 61.0 cm\n   Maple Ridge  mean = (42+78+50+70+88+35+65+55+80+47)/10 = 610/10 = 61.0 cm\n   The means are identical.\n2. Cedar Hollow deviations from 61: 3, 1, 1, 3, 2, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0. Sum = 14. MAD = 14/10 = 1.4 cm\n   Maple Ridge deviations from 61: 19, 17, 11, 9, 27, 26, 4, 6, 19, 14. Sum = 152. MAD = 152/10 = 15.2 cm\n   Maple Ridge is about 11 times more variable than Cedar Hollow.\n3. Cedar Hollow dot plot clusters tightly from 58 to 64. Maple Ridge dot plot spreads from 35 to 88 with no obvious cluster.\n4. Disagree (or agree with caveat). The CENTERS are the same — the typical height is similar. But the VARIABILITY is wildly different. Cedar Hollow's saplings are very uniform. Maple Ridge's are inconsistent — some are much taller AND some are much shorter than the mean. The 'typical' description hides this.\n5. Maple Ridge is the better bet for the tallest sapling next year. Its current max is 88 cm; Cedar Hollow's max is 64 cm. Even though the means agree, Maple Ridge's spread reaches higher.",
